//? Title: Montgomery arithmetic
//? Subtitle: Reusable modulus setup, actual REDC carries and binary powers
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Repeated products modulo a fixed odd integer can spend substantial work on
reductions. Montgomery arithmetic changes representation so its reduced-domain
product uses multiplication, addition and shifts. We derive the reduction,
observe the actual carry that a full-width sum needs, retain the original
arithmetic and toy RSA examples, and count real domain conversions separately
from the binary powering loop.
)

\toc

> Derive reduction before using it

Let R=2 to power64 and n be odd. Precompute nprime=−n inverse modulo R. For
T=a*b with both factors less than n, define q as the low64 bits of T times
nprime. Then T+q*n is divisible by R. Its quotient u is less than2n because T<n
squared<nR and q<R. Thus subtracting n at most once gives a canonical residue
congruent to T times R inverse modulo n.

$$
q=(T\bmod R)n'\bmod R,\qquad u=(T+qn)/R,\qquad \operatorname{REDC}(T)=u-n\,[u\ge n].
$$
\alttext(The correction makes the low64 sum bits zero; division by R is a shift and one subtraction completes reduction.)

The sum can require129 bits even though each product fits128. The canonical
kernel retains the carry from the unsigned128-bit addition separately. If it is
set, the quotient is R plus its stored low64 bits and the final subtraction is
required. Unsigned subtraction of n then gives the correct result below n,
including this carry case. Discarding that bit before comparison was a
full-width defect in the legacy routine.

> A small actual reduction

Modulo17, R is congruent to1, so a REDC product of3 and4 is12. The diagram reads
the producer's actual product, correction and final residue. This convenient
modulus makes the arithmetic hand-checkable; the representation change is
nontrivial for most other moduli.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/montgomery_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    dm::algorithms::MontgomeryContext c(17);
    auto t = c.reduce_trace(3, 4);
    dm::graphics::Diagram d;
    std::print("| n | nprime | product high | product low | q | carry | quotient low | subtract | result " "|\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- | ---: | --- | ---: |\n| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} " "| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",
               c.modulus(), c.nprime(), t.product_high, t.product_low,
               t.correction, t.sum_carry, t.quotient_low, t.subtracted, t.result);
    d.node("a", {0, 0},
           std::format("Actual product\n{} * {} = {}", t.left, t.right,
                       t.product_low)).node("b", {7, 0},
                                            std::format("Actual correction q\n{}",
                                                        t.correction)).node(
        "c", {14, 0},
        std::format("Actual quotient / result\n{} / {}", t.quotient_low,
                    t.result)).edge("a", "b").edge("b", "c");
    d.save_svg("build/montgomery_tiny.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| n | nprime | product high | product low | q | carry | quotient low | subtract | result |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- | ---: | --- | ---: |
| 17 | 1085102592571150095 | 0 | 12 | 13021231110853801140 | false | 12 | false | 12 |
\image(figures/montgomery_tiny.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual small-modulus reduction, including its correction.)
\alttext(Three observed stages show the product, correction and reduced result12.)

> Setup once and keep the loop in its domain

Represent x by xR modulo n. Multiplying two representations with REDC yields
xyR, so repeated operations stay in the same domain. Enter with REDC((x modulo
n)*(R squared modulo n)); leave with REDC(xR*1). The context stores nprime, R
modulo n and R squared modulo n. Setup and reduction of arbitrary inputs use
remainder operations. Consequently the historical convenience function, which
constructs a fresh context for every ordinary product, does not avoid division
over the whole call.

For odd n, n squared is1 modulo8. Starting the inverse iteration at n therefore
gives at least three correct bits. Each Newton update v←v(2−nv) doubles the
number, so five updates suffice for64 bits. The figure displays check words in
units of1e18; its table retains exact integers. The next program displays actual
setup iterates for the original modulus3233 and performs the original two
ordinary products. Counts of low-level CPU instructions are deliberately not
inferred from these abstract operations.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/montgomery_setup.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    dm::algorithms::MontgomeryContext c(3233);
    std::vector<double> x, y;
    auto v = c.inverse_steps();
    std::print("| actual Newton stage | inverse word | n times inverse modulo R |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: " "|\n");
    for (std::size_t k = 0; k < v.size(); ++k) {
        auto check = std::uint64_t(c.modulus() * v[k]);
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} |\n", k, v[k], check);
        x.push_back(k);
        y.push_back(double(check) / 1e18);
    }
    std::print("\nOriginal products: {}, {}.\n",
               dm::algorithms::montgomery_multiply(123, 456, 1000003),
               dm::algorithms::montgomery_multiply(9999, 8888, 100000007));
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.points(x, y, "scaled check word", {37, 99, 235}, 6).title(
        "Montgomery setup: actual Newton inverse checks").x_label(
        "seed and successive refinement index").y_label("check word / 1e18").save_svg("build/montgomery_setup.svg");
}
```
