> Retain the original toy RSA calculation

The original arithmetic example uses p=61, q=53, n=3233, e=17 and d=2753.
Encrypting123 gives855 and powering855 to2753 returns123. Here one context is
reused and each full power enters and leaves the Montgomery domain only once.
The trace reports domain residues, which must not be read as ordinary residues.
This tiny unpadded calculation illustrates modular arithmetic and provides no
secure encryption scheme.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/montgomery_rsa.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    dm::algorithms::MontgomeryContext c(3233);
    auto t = c.power_trace(123, 17);
    auto back = c.power(t.result, 2753);
    std::vector<double> x, y;
    std::print("Ciphertext: {}. Recovered message: {}. Actual REDC calls for encryption: {}.\n\n| bit | " "remaining | running domain residue | accumulator domain residue | squared domain residue " "|\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n",
               t.result, back, t.reductions);
    for (std::size_t k = 0; k < t.steps.size(); ++k) {
        auto &s = t.steps[k];
        x.push_back(k);
        y.push_back(s.accumulator_after);
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", k, s.remaining, s.running,
                   s.accumulator_after, s.squared);
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.points(x, y, "domain accumulator", {37, 99, 235}, 6).title(
        "Original toy RSA: domain states").x_label("bit index, low first").y_label("stored domain residue").save_svg("build/montgomery_rsa.svg");
}
```
